(unreleased): The Programmer's Learning Machine is back to life, in version 4.0 this time

  * Modernize:
    - Run on modern JDK by not using Thread.stop().  [Juan Manuel Méndez Rey]
      If the student writes an infinite loop, this won't get stopped, but at least the PLM can compile.
    - Modernize the build chain by using maven  [Juan Manuel Méndez Rey]
    - Modernize the C lang; student's printfs are now shown in the console
    - C lang: use the Address Sanitizer rather than valgrind: it's ways faster and we control the compilation anyway.
    - Scala: Move from Scala2 to Scala 3.9
    - Move from junit4 to junit5.

  * Simplify:
    - Kill ExerciseTemplatingEntity. The design is so much simpler without it.

  * Fix bugs:
    - Make sure we don't try to sign git commits even if the user local configuration requests otherwise.
    - Only log error messages, not the deprecation messages from the PLM itself.  [Juan Manuel Méndez Rey]
    - #450, #523: Code was always reported as successful in C, even if it did not compile.

  (TBC)

  This release is the first major PLM release since over 10 years, so I feel like I (Mt) need to explain a bit how we arrived
  here. This text is a bit personal and maybe offtopic, so nobody forces you to read it if you're not interested :)

  The story of the PLM is not completely linear. This software was started in 2008 when I got frustrated from my other big
  project (SimGrid) which is technically very challenging. In some sense, I work on the PLM when I get fed up with the SimGrid
  challenges. The PLM was initialy written with Gérald Oster for our students at ESIAL, a graduate school for IT engineers. 
  
  In 2014, It was working so well that we got a funding from Inria to modernize and extend this software. One important goal was
  to allow the teachers to efficiently help their students during a PLM session by converting a heavy client in Java into a
  webapp in Scala. We hired an engineer and started the conversion, but it turned out to be much more complicated than expected. 
  
  The technical debt was much higher than expected. Turning a single user tool into a multi-user system was difficult. Executing
  the student code in remote shared executors was difficult. Orchestring the dockerized services was difficult. Rendering the
  outcome of remote executions was difficult. In addition, we did several design mistakes. We started the conversion too quickly
  without first clearing the technical debt. We went for a fancy but experimental web framework, and added many neat
  dependencies that resulted in a sort of technological firework (and dependency puzzle). Finally, I was hired by another
  institution in between, and I don't even use the PLM in my new job. At the end, the PLM webapp (v3) was never really usable.

  In 2017, I released v2.7 (The Pragmatic and thus Despicable release), trying to restart the development of the v2 branch to
  build on a codebase that I at least understand. It was a tiny release only acknowledging the fact that v3 was a dead project.
  For almost 10 years, I was sad that I could not salvage the v3. I tried a reimplementation from scratch in Rust with a group
  of students, but it did not went through. I tried to motivate colleagues to join me in this effort, but understandably nobody
  wants to invest time in a dead project. I tried twice to get the PLM exercises adopted by another platform that would be
  maintained, but the exercise base heavily depends on didactic features that are very specific to the PLM (see PEDAGOGY.md).

  In 2026, a new bug was opened against the PLM: https://bugs.debian.org/1139926 Thread.stop, on which the PLM v2 depends, is
  removed from Java 21, sentencing the project. It came right in time, as I was exhausted of SimGrid because of a bug that I was
  hunting since over 6 months in vain. When the bug 1139926 was reported against the PLM, I jumped on the occasion.

  It was also a great excuse to experiment with coding assistants. I am politically opposed the societal project that the big
  companies are trying to push with the gen IA tools, but I was also very curious. I am still wondering whether another gen IA
  is possible, one that is less destructive for its users, their society, and our planet, or whether that technology is doomed
  to be an individualistic plague, only intended to give a strong grip on our minds to the techno-fascists that are threatening
  our future.

  I wanted to experiment on how Claude Code could help me salvaging the PLM from its technical debt (a dead project cannot
  suffer). I asked for a technical documentation of the compilation workflow, and the doc was rather good: perfectly
  enlightening how messy the situation was. I iterated 5 or 10 weeks, asking for small patches that change both the code and the
  technical doc, reviewing everything and rewriting a lot myself. I managed to get it done. I got rid of the technical debt in
  the PLM. The compilation workflow is now rather clean and well tested, the C language is working well, the data mutability was
  pushed back to acceptable levels, and the Game singleton is only used in the UI. On Linux, Mac and Windows.

  Given my ideological opposition to the societal project of gen AI companies, I refuse to give them one dime and used a free
  plan, with a cooldown period of 5 hours when the free tokens are exhausted. I had to come up with an efficient workflow. I
  draft my prompts in a separate file, I systematically refuse the suggestion for a next step and stick to my plan instead. I do
  copy these suggestions in my Prompt file, to review them during the reflexive sessions that I run during the cooldown between
  the coding sessions. I tried to save tokens to robot, by requesting small patches that are not perfect. This gives me the
  opportunity to review and rework this code myself. The tool can actually be used to do the job, even if you sometimes get the
  feeling to ride a drunk slug, that keeps forgetting the plan and includes really stupid ideas within its shiny code.

  It turned out to be a really intense period. At some point, I had 4 work sessions per day on average despite the 5 hours
  cooldown period, sometimes even 5 sessions a day (opening the computer in the middle of the night just to type "continue" on
  the previous prompt that had failed to complete in time, and then struggling to fall back asleep). Between the work sessions,
  I was furbishing my prompts, reviewing and organizing the suggestions collected previously, reflecting on the strategies to
  get the most out of the tool, and deciding what to do next. All this was really exhausting because I was doing that in
  addition to my usual work as a full professor at the university. It was also a very interesting period, very instructive on
  the addictive nature of this technology. Friends started to ask why I was inflicting this to myself, but I was hooked. It's a
  bit of https://xkcd.com/242/ but I also have a very strong incentive from my professional milieu to get acquainted to the gen
  IA technology despite my initial conscientious objection, so that I can teach about it. So I kept taking the Kool Aid, just to
  understand what I could do to the tool and what it would do to me.

  At some point, a colleague felt pity and gave me an access to the team plan she had for free for her research team. It really
  helped to alleviate the addiction. Since I have more token that my methods could possibly use, I can have only one work
  sessions or two per day. I still don't get enough sleep, but at least my nights are not interrupted anymore. I still strive to
  save tokens, to at least not waste this manna that burns the planet, but also because it's an efficient way to review and
  integrate the tool output.

  As an experiment, I tried to use that version of the robot to design and implement a PLM lesson on the application layer of
  networking. This topic is challenging to teach because it is technically demanding for both the students and the teachers. The
  students cannot think in isolation but must work with an environment through an (obscure) API. The feedback they get from that
  environment is scarce when it exists, making it difficult for the students to build an useful mental representation of what's
  going on. As a teacher, I was somewhat happy of my lectures but much less of the practical sessions. The network varies and
  fail so any harness I could build was failing faster that the student code it was supposed to test, while coding without
  harness within the time limits of practical sessions could only lead on hollow and uninteresting tasks.

  To my surprise, I managed to get a set of very nice exercises on these notions, where the student has to interact with
  simulated servers to discover the IRC, DNS, XMPP, SMTP, HTTP and FTP protocols, to fully get the grasp on the message framing
  issue. The used network API has exactly the same syntax and semantic than the idiomatic network API of each language. Each
  server realistically implements a subset of the protocol. The documentation is instructive, if not nice looking. The graphical
  representation of the situation helps building an effective mental representation. The simulated servers provide increadibly
  affectionate error messages compared to real implementations. I even scripted the exercises to give the students to interact
  with scripted bots and make the exercise less ingrate.
  
  I could not think of a better way to teach the networking API to my students. 

  But I was struggling with the idea of advertising to my students the gen IA that I'd prefer to object myself. How could I tell
  my students to handle IA as a strong drug (somehow related to a legal cocaine) and to not use it before 25 years, if I was a
  consumer myself? I decided to split the roles with the robot. It generates the code from my prompt and I review it before
  integration to the git. I also sharpen manually the technical documentation from its drafts. For the learning material
  presented to the students, we do the opposite. I write it manually and the robot is only to diagnose technical inacuracy and
  didactic inconsistancies. I also write the PEDAGOGY file that gives the teacher documentation. It's not perfect (and really
  time consuming because the robot shows no mercy in its reviews), but this approach cannot be mistaken with an advertisment for
  the technology by my students. In case you wonder, I teach in a very specific institution where over 90% of our undergrads
  become academic researchers. Currently, maybe one quarter of our undergrad students are conscientious objectors while graduated
  students seem to all wov to not use gen IA for there studies, to better understand what they are taught.

  I would not say that the coding assistant saved me any time ever. It allowed me to code things that I could not dream of
  coding manually (no time for that), but it keeps side-questing me. Right now, I am supposed to write a XMPP client from
  scratch to rewrite the draft of an mission text that was generated (rewriting almost everything is needed both for my ethical
  point of view but also to fit my standards in learning material), but instead I added colored markers and underlining to
  represent the compilers' error messages in the editor, code completion and I'm considering adding a full featured debugger. I
  think that the coding assistant almost never saved me any time, but instead trapped me into doing things that are neat and
  useless. Some say it's a skill issue, I'd say that when your favorite tool is a screw driver, you lose interest in nails.

  I will continue to use a coding assistant for the implementation of the PLM as long as I have an access, but I will always use
  it as if my access could be closed tomorrow. I want ensure that I could get back at writing each line of change next month.
  
  I very reluctant to use it in SimGrid, for two reasons. First, SimGrid is so hard to write that I'm afraid that the robot
  could too easily fool me into lousy plans. The architecture and code of the PLM is simple enough that I can keep a clear
  vision of the path I want to take. Working on a model-checker that can formally verify unmodified POSIX applications at
  industrial quality grade (it's the pitch of McSimGrid, my current work within SimGrid) is outrageously complicated. Something
  nobody ever attempted before afaik. I could use the power of a coding assistant, but I'm affraid that a powerful coding tool
  could bring more complexity (rings and bells) that I could handle manually once the tool gets unavailable.
  
  Second, SimGrid is a research project and I'm affraid that a coding assistant could drain my creativity and restrain me to
  middling solutions. These tools are said to liberate their users of chore of the tactical programing, to save time for
  strategic thinking, but I'm not sure about it. When your favorite tool is a screw driver, you lose interest in nails. I'm not
  sure at all that we would have written SimGrid 25 years ago if we had coding assistants at that time. We were initially not
  interested in writing a simulator of distributed systems in the first place, but we needed such a tool to test our scientific
  contributions. As we intially disregarded the importance of this piece of software, we wrote it in a rush without a proper
  literature review. In particular, we did not knew of discrete event simulation (DES) at that time. But that's a good news
  because the main feature of SimGrid (computing the performance contention of data streams that compete over the resources)
  would have been impossible in a DES where the events are supposed independent: congestion is not explicitely modeled in other
  network simulators, but emerge from the simulation at microscopic scale of network packets. Not knowing about this commonplace
  allowed us to prove it wrong. Accuracy in network simulation is possible without computer-intensive detailed simulations,
  provided a careful work to validate and calibrate the models. Retrospectively, I think that we'd have instead vibe coded a
  middling simulator, but with an impressive graphical interface greatly increasing our publication productivity...

  Sorry to be so verbose on my motivations and on SimGrid in the PLM ChangeLog, but it's important for me to be very explicit
  about my feeling on coding assistants (see also the "Why to learn programming when I could vibe code it" section of the
  PEDAGOGY document). My opinion is now that these tools may be OK if you are more interested in using the program than in how
  it's built inside (provided that you're not a conscientious objector), but using them to do your work seems very toxic to me.
  You'll soon lose your professional habilities because of these leeches.

